module gunbc.witness_row_cost

data witness_row_cost_spine_note: String = "Per-witness cost receipts (placement workstream #5 spine). THE RECORD is std.observation.ObservationEvent (progress-observation-design §2/§3c) — not a TSV schema and not WitnessTimingRow. Production shape is N+1 per entry: exactly ONE entry resolve-phase Concluded event (entry-level/shared resolve wall) plus one declaration-grain Concluded event per witness (eval wall + actual outcome; Refused distinct from Failed). RSS is MeasuredUnavailable with an explicit cause. The canonical event and joined receipt retain exact Nanosecond eval, resolve, and warm values; histogram, ordering, and attribution consume those exact values. The TSV/drift/placement wires are named Millisecond presentation and policy boundaries and may floor only there. The resolve column repeats the observed full entry resolve for each child — never divided across witnesses and never summed N times into a corpus total. W2 filters the SAME events via AttentionLevel/ObservationDensity (Ambient may suppress a render line while the complete receipt still retains the row). Basis values MUST cite an arm64 srv-fleet run id (never local x86). A row with no dated basis is BasisAbsent. Drift is falsifier-cadence counted, not merge-refusing. Executing consumers: dag/test/claim/witness_row_cost_drift_witness_test.dag and dag/test/claim/witness_row_cost_projection_witness_test.dag."

data coverage_is_a_join_of_two_instruments_note: String = "WHAT A ROSTER CAN DETECT AND WHICH ROWS GET TO TRY ARE DIFFERENT FACTS, AND NEITHER ALONE IS A COVERAGE STATEMENT (derived 2026-08-18, sharp-raven-273, from two instruments run against the same corpus on the same day). A MUTATION BATTERY answers the first: break the thing a row discriminates, run the row, and see whether it reds -- executed in a session, where rows run to completion. A BUDGET-REFUSAL CENSUS answers the second: which enrolled rows were killed on their cost budget before they finished. A row that is sharp in a dispatch and killed on budget in CI is UNDISCRIMINATING IN CI, however good the row is, so a battery alone overstates coverage and a budget census alone says nothing about whether the surviving rows discriminate anything. The coverage claim is the JOIN: rows that both complete and red when their subject breaks. MEASURED, both halves: a four-cluster battery found exactly one mutation that reds nothing where its cluster should discriminate it (a dangling DeclarationRef bind), while the same day's main run reported 131 BUDGET-REFUSED identities -- among them all 28 rows of the decl_facts reflection cluster, at 2.1-2.3s against the ceiling then in force. So that cluster's battery result and its CI coverage disagree completely, which is the whole point of stating this. TWO DISCIPLINES THE BATTERY HALF NEEDS, both learned by getting them wrong first. An entry passed with no function selector runs ZERO rows and reports zero failures, which is indistinguishable from a cluster that survived every mutation -- an empty run is not a green run, and it produced three false 'no data' lines here before it was caught. And REDS-NOTHING IS NOT FAILS-TO-DISCRIMINATE: a construction census that ignores a changed field value, or an edge claim that ignores a changed function body, is answering the question it declared, so counting those as coverage holes inflates the artifact with defects nobody claimed -- four of the seven zeros measured here were scope, not gaps. THE THRESHOLD SIDE IS DELIBERATELY NOT NAMED IN THIS ROW: witness_row_cost_migration_threshold_note derives it (gunbc_ci_fast_lane_eval_budget_ms / order-of-magnitude), and restating a number here would be the second hand-maintained copy DESIGN section 3 forbids. What this row asserts is only that cost refusal and detection power are orthogonal axes and a coverage claim needs both."

data witness_decomposition_does_not_reduce_entry_cost_note: String = "DO NOT SPLIT A WITNESS EXPECTING ENTRY STARTUP TO GET CHEAPER (operator design note, 2026-08-05). Per-witness EVAL cost and per-entry RESOLVE/materialization cost are different terms, and this receipt carries them in different columns for exactly that reason. Splitting one witness fn into several does not divide the entry's resolve — it AMORTIZES one expensive resolve across more rows, so the per-row figure falls while the corpus cost does not. Reading a drop in per-row eval as a saving is therefore a measurement artifact of the split itself. MEASURED, on falsifier run 30824310833: 682 of 930 entries resolve at 1000ms or more (max 11223ms), and corpus totals are 1638s resolve against 3987s eval, the latter dominated by roughly a dozen enrolled behavioral receipts. The resolve column repeats the observed full entry resolve for each child (witness_row_cost_spine_note) precisely so it can never be divided across witnesses and mistaken for a per-row cost. THE REPEATED-RESOLVE TERM IS NOT THIS LANE'S: it belongs to the shared-floor / materialization program, where one index amortizes across many entries. Witness decomposition is justified by what a witness PROVES — one behavior per claim, discriminating REDs — never by an expected startup saving it cannot deliver."

data witness_repeated_crossing_cost_class_note: String = "REPEATED FRONTEND / EVALUATOR / EMITTER CROSSINGS ARE A COST CLASS OF THEIR OWN, and the remedy is ONE CROSSING PER MECHANISM (2026-08-19, quiet-bee-353, dispatched off the floor-cut cost triage in v2.workflow.floor_expected_red). THE SHAPE: a witness entry establishes one mechanism over several fixtures — a grammar admits these shapes and refuses those, an emitter lowers this construct to that text — and it is written as one claim per fixture. Each claim evaluates in its OWN frame by design, so each one reconstructs the whole apparatus ahead of the fixture: dag_lex_rules() and a 6,230-line dag_grammar() for three lines of probe source, or a full v1 frontend-and-emitter compile for a five-line module. The fixtures are irreducible; the reconstruction is not, and it is the entire cost. THIS IS NOT THE SIBLING CLASS ABOVE. witness_decomposition_does_not_reduce_entry_cost_note rules that SPLITTING a witness cannot divide the entry resolve, and that decomposition is justified by what a claim PROVES rather than by a startup saving. Both hold here and neither is weakened: what is removed is repeated construction WITHIN one entry, the claim population is not being traded for a cheaper per-row figure, and every assertion survives. The test that separates the two is whether the rows prove DIFFERENT mechanisms or ONE mechanism over different fixtures — the first must stay separate whatever it costs, the second was never N claims, it was one claim written N times. THE REMEDY, in order of preference: build the apparatus once inside a claim, bind it, and fold the fixture population through it; where a host primitive fuses construction with assertion — compile_dag_rust_emit_check is memo-keyed on its include and exclude lists as well as its source, so two claims naming one source with different lists are two full compiles — express the whole conjunction in one call rather than one call per token. WHAT IT MEASURED, so the class is a cost figure rather than a preference (release claim_batch, one remote executor, 2026-08-19): dag/test/claim/long/e0308_mechanical_trio_test.dag went from nine witnesses in 2450ms to five in 1346ms, four of the deleted rows being negated restatements of an exclude list a sibling claim already carried more strongly. THAT PAIR IS COMMENSURABLE AND THE NEXT ONE IS NOT, which has to be said rather than averaged over: both e0308 figures come from this instrument with e0308 as the FIRST entry of its dispatch, so both paid whatever process-scoped first-touch fill the run owes, and the difference is the witnesses. dag/test/claim/long/emitted_lib_rs_module_declaration_witness_test.dag ran three compiles and now runs two, both green — but its before-figure is a CI floor observation and its after-figure, 561ms, was taken from a WARM position behind e0308 in the same dispatch, so the two wall numbers are not each other's control and only the crossing count is established. A PER-WITNESS COST FIGURE IS NOT A PROPERTY OF ITS WITNESS WHEN AN UNREPORTED LAZY SHARED FILL IS IN PLAY (the receipt is in v2.workflow.floor_expected_red: a relocation moved a 45s module-path-index first touch onto an untouched neighbour, 746ms to 45941ms, repaired in gunbc#8470), and a fold changes which claim touches what first — so a fold that appears not to pay may be one whose saving landed on another row, and a fold that appears to pay may have pushed a fill onto someone else. Fold measurements are therefore only readable when the folded entry holds the same position in both runs. AND ONE SUBJECT WHERE THE SAME REASONING WAS REFUTED BY THE SAME INSTRUMENT, which is why this paragraph reports a measurement rather than a rule. dag/test/claim/long/dag_arrow_lambda_witness_test.dag looked like the flagship: nineteen dag_grammar() constructions serving thirteen three-line sources, one 6,230-line grammar per claim, and the cost triage that dispatched this work named it as the specimen. Folding it to two claims was written, run, and DID NOT PAY — 210.9s for the two folded claims against roughly 208s for the fourteen it replaced. Solving for the terms, a dag_grammar() construction is about 0.5s and a single three-line parse through the interpreted frontend is about 12s, so the apparatus was never the cost and the fixtures always were. The consolidation was REVERTED rather than kept on a cheaper-looking claim count: it traded per-source failure locality across thirteen fixtures for about four percent, and keeping it while its stated justification was refuted is the rung inflation DESIGN §4b names. WHAT THE REFUTATION ACTUALLY FOUND is a frontend cost-shape defect — twelve seconds to parse three lines — which is not a witness defect and does not belong to this class; it is recorded here because the measurement that was supposed to price this class is the only place it has been observed. THE STANDING LESSON: repeated construction is a HYPOTHESIS about where an entry's cost sits, not a reading of it, and the fold is only justified once the apparatus is measured to dominate the fixtures. Fold second, measure first. TWO MORE SHAPES THE SAME PASS FOUND, in the quarantined execution family under src/v2/test/claim/execution/long. THE STAIRCASE: a frontend probed stage by stage, one claim per stage, each claim redoing every stage before its own — add_arrow_eval_by_execution had tokenize, parse, normalize and resolve as four claims costing ten stage-runs, and the deepest claim already fails on any earlier stage's refusal, so the three shallow ones asserted nothing it does not. THE INDEX SWEEP: two claims over one source at one stage differing only in an array index, each running the whole pipeline to reach it — pick_ingested_probe's then-arm and else-arm magnitude rows, now one bound resolved module and two assertions. AND THE EXACT DUPLICATE, which is the class at its most embarrassing and appeared twice: dag_add_emit_add_fn_accepts_holds was a call to its sibling, and add_arrow_eval_produced_add_body_executes_holds had a body byte-identical to add_arrow_eval_source_driven_add_executes_holds. All five deletions are receipted in the gunbc.witness_deferral_freeze shrink log; that roster's monotonicity gate refuses GROWTH, so a fold there must keep one of the existing identities rather than mint a name for the merged row, which is why the survivors are named for the deepest claim rather than for the merged mechanism. WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY LEFT ALONE, named so the boundary is not rediscovered: the paired emit-vs-eval rows (emit_host_*, bind_eval_by_execution, loop_eval_by_execution) run different oracles, different expected values or different fixtures against one subject, which is discrimination and not repetition. What repeats in bind and loop is FIXTURE CONSTRUCTION — table_fixture_bind_arrow_with_body and its siblings, one source-to-arrow pipeline per claim — and that is not a witness defect: the rows genuinely need the fixture and genuinely assert different things about it. Folding them would buy a cheaper row by deleting a discriminating one. That residue belongs to whatever gives a frame a shared prepared subject, which is the closure-pollution and materialization territory with its own owner, not to this class. THE COST OF THE REMEDY, stated because it is real: a folded claim names the mechanism, not the fixture, so a failure reports the claim and the failing fixture is read from the fold. That is why each folded population is a named list saying what it asserts."

data witness_row_cost_clock_basis_note: String = "THE EVAL COLUMN IS WALL AND THE FAST-LANE CAP IS ENFORCED ON THREAD CPU — two clocks, and a threshold built on one selects a different population than the other kills. The column is named eval_wall_ms so the basis is visible rather than implied. The bound that keeps the wall figure useful: eval is single-threaded, so cpu is bounded above by wall, and a wall figure UNDER the cap therefore PROVES cpu under it. That is the firing direction of every dissolve-on trigger stated as 'lands under the fast-lane budget', so those stay decidable on the recorded number. The blind direction is ranking — how near the cap a row sits, and whether an over-cap wall figure reflects cpu at all — which is what a per-witness cost envelope needs. A row AT or PAST the cap is a censored measurement rather than a cost: its value is where the kill landed and the true figure is unbounded above. SEED STATUS: the seed receipt now records both clocks (gunbc#7820) but says which is which by FIELD NAME, which the 2026-08-05 operator ruling replaces with a basis carried on the measurement itself (ClockBasis = CpuClock | WallClock, and a basis on TimedOut so a crossing names its cap). DISSOLVE-ON: that authority lands in std.observation and this projection carries cpu and wall through it without confusing them."

data witness_row_cost_basis_host_class_note: String = "Every signed basis row names host_class=srv_fleet_arm64 (the same idiom as gunbc_ci_floor_batch_clamp_note's 1000ms coefficient basis). When the fleet or governor width moves, basis rows re-sign from a new cited run — never a local measurement."

data placement_workstream_task_20_open_unknown_note: String = "TASK #20 — REOPENED 2026-07-26 as OpenUnknown (was marked completed-but-unexplained when a red vanished on merging main without diagnosis — an un-diagnosed defect wearing a green hat). STATUS: OpenUnknown. REPRODUCTION (start from arm64 srv fleet, do not re-derive on local x86 — this container proves logic, never cost): begin from the gate_gauntlet_flip_basis_note composition run 30181042050 on srv fleet arm64; reproduce the per-witness cost receipt + drift comparator path on the falsifier cadence, reading the run's floor-receipts artifact — floor-witness-row-cost-receipt.tsv and floor-witness-row-cost-drift-receipt.tsv — plus their [receipt] summary lines. CORRECTED: this protocol used to say to collect [witness-row-cost] and [witness-row-cost-drift] LOG lines. The drift tag now has zero emit sites and the row bodies are no longer mirrored to stderr on any cadence, so the old instruction would return nothing; the receipts themselves are unchanged and now leave the runner as an artifact (gunbc.ci_workflow ci_floor_receipts_upload_step). The original red's mechanism was never named before the merge went green. DISSOLVE-ON: the red is reproduced OR its absence is explained by a typed, cited mechanism row — never silently closed again."

data expectation_frontier_burn_down_lane_note: String = "TRACKED LANE (not this PR's scope): the expectation frontier is measurable but unshrunk — 340 service-op call sites, 16 annotated, 67 undeclared dispatches on generated_artifact_drift_test alone (expectation_declaration_frontier_note). The three A2 annotations in this PR (VerifyBuildArtifacts, chmod, shell.Exec.Run read-back) are the first deliberate shrink sites; the burn-down continues as its own lane with the [expectation-frontier] receipt as the progress oracle."

data witness_cost_native_production_note: String = "The former hand-Rust timing-row materialized view dissolved when its trigger fired: production now constructs the canonical N+1 ObservationEvent stream through the authored constructors below and executes project_witness_cost_receipt. The accepted WitnessCostProjection is the sole semantic row input to TSV, drift, slowest attribution, and histogram consumers. The seed bridge transports only occurrence facts it already measured (entry, declaration identity, outcome, duration); role classification, parent/child joining, duplicate detection, warm derivation, and refusal all remain here."

data witness_cost_transition_extract_note: String = "ObservationTransition is read only through witness_cost_concluded_outcome (Optional extract). reusable Begin|Step|Concluded-to-Bool predicates are dissolved (review 43351). Event role (entry resolve parent vs declaration witness) is witness_cost_event_role — a typed Optional<gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostEventRole> extract, not is_* Bool nicknames (review 43409 dissolves the review 43383/43399 Bool-filter Scaffold). Subject segment fields are read through std.observation observation_subject_entry_path / observation_subject_declaration / observation_subject_phase_name / observation_subject_module_path (canonical accessors; review 43361/43409) — no local ObservationSegment walkers."

data witness_cost_event_role_note: String = "WitnessCostEventRole is the single classification authority for the N+1 join (review 43409). EntryResolveParent requires entry-level subject shape: EntrySegment present, ModuleSegment Absent, DeclarationSegment Absent, PhaseSegment name=resolve, Concluded — so a declaration- or module-nested resolve phase cannot corrupt the parent count. DeclarationWitness requires DeclarationGrain + Concluded. project_witness_cost_receipt matches the Optional role extract into WitnessCostProjection arms (review 43429 — no has_*/is_* Bool walker over the role; duplicate parents refuse via witness_cost_check_unique_resolve_parent → WitnessCostProjectionRefused)."


type WitnessRowCostDatedBasis {
  clock: std.measure.ClockBasis
  eval: std.measure.Millisecond
  run_ref: String
}

type WitnessRowCostVerdict
  = WithinBasis
  | DriftExceeded { observed: std.measure.Millisecond, basis: std.measure.Millisecond }
  | BasisAbsent
  | BasisClockMismatch { observed: std.measure.ClockBasis, basis: std.measure.ClockBasis }

data witness_row_cost_clock_mismatch_note: String = "THE WALL THIS WHOLE LANE EXISTS TO BUILD. A drift verdict compares an observed duration against a dated one, and before the clock became data nothing in the types said the two were read from the same clock — so comparing a thread-CPU figure against a wall baseline typechecked, produced a confident WithinBasis or DriftExceeded, and was wrong in a way no test could distinguish from right. That is not hypothetical: the fast-lane cap is enforced on thread CPU while every cost receipt recorded wall, and four sessions spent an evening comparing 1005 against 5004 and 2507 against 5003 without either number being incorrect. BasisClockMismatch is a FOURTH ARM and not a Bool collapse, for the same reason std.content_hash compare_content_hash answers ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable rather than false: an incomparable pair is neither within nor exceeding, and rendering it as either invents a verdict. It is also not BasisAbsent — a basis IS present, it is simply measured on a different clock, and the remedy differs (seed the missing basis versus fix the producer that handed over the wrong clock). WHY THE THRESHOLD SIDE STAYS WALL: operator ruling 2026-08-05 closed the witness-grain denominator as WALL, with thread CPU retained as the REMEDY DISCRIMINATOR rather than a second threshold arm — high wall with high CPU is algorithm or repeated evaluation, high wall with low CPU is waiting, I/O, subprocess or scheduling. So a CPU-clocked basis row is not forbidden by this type, it is simply not what the witness threshold is stated on, and handing one to this comparator against a wall observation now REFUSES instead of answering. WHERE THIS WALL RUNS, stated so the rung is neither inflated nor understated (DESIGN section 4b). It is reached in the witness corpus by dag/test/claim/witness_row_cost_drift_witness_test.dag AND on the production falsifier cadence, which is the half that took a second pass: the seed's write_witness_row_cost_drift_receipt_at used to call only witness_row_cost_exceeds_basis, the bare ratio predicate, and rebuild DriftExceeded / WithinBasis / BasisAbsent as Rust string literals -- a second representation of this sum, and one the clock never crossed, so a cross-clock pair on the cadence would still have answered. That comparison was correct at the time only because both sides happened to be wall, which is a fact about the producers rather than a property of the mechanism, and nothing about its greenness depended on the mechanism being right. It now calls witness_row_cost_seed_verdict_dated / _undated and RENDERS THE ARM THE AUTHORITY NAMED, the basis files carry a required per-row clock cell (an unstated or unmodelled clock refuses at parse, never defaults to wall), and the Rust arm reconstruction is DELETED rather than kept beside this module -- a climb dissolves the lower-rung production machinery it obsoletes. Proven end to end by claim_executor's drift_verdicts_come_from_the_authority_and_a_cross_clock_basis_refuses, which asserts the refusal on BOTH sides of the 2x ratio so it is decided by the clock and not by the magnitude, and which is the only consumer that crosses this Rust seam at all: the .dag witnesses cannot reach it and the parser tests only show the cell is read. THE ONE ARM STILL DECIDED SEED-SIDE is ObservationAbsent, for a row that never executed, and deliberately: an absent OBSERVATION has no counterpart in this sum, so giving it one would let a row with no measurement reach a comparator at all."

fn witness_row_cost_drift_ratio_numerator() -> std.nat.Nat {
  2
}

fn witness_row_cost_exceeds_basis(observed: std.measure.Millisecond, basis: std.measure.Millisecond) -> Bool {
  millisecond_count(m: observed) > millisecond_count(m: basis) * witness_row_cost_drift_ratio_numerator()
}

fn witness_row_cost_verdict(
  observed: std.measure.Millisecond,
  observed_clock: std.measure.ClockBasis,
  basis: Optional<gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessRowCostDatedBasis>,
) -> gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessRowCostVerdict {
  match basis {
    Absent => BasisAbsent
    Present { value: b } =>
      if !clock_basis_eq(a: observed_clock, b: b.clock) {
        BasisClockMismatch { observed: observed_clock, basis: b.clock }
      } else if witness_row_cost_exceeds_basis(observed: observed, basis: b.eval) {
        DriftExceeded { observed: observed, basis: b.eval }
      } else {
        WithinBasis
      }
  }
}

data witness_row_cost_migration_threshold_note: String = "MANDATORY-MIGRATION DISCLOSURE (operator ruling 2026-08-04, dispatched via keen-ibex-349): 5000ms (gunbc_ci_fast_lane_eval_budget_ms, v2.workflow.ci_floor_plan) is a FAIL-STOP protecting the executor — never a budget, tolerance, or target; ordinary witnesses test narrow business logic and belong sub-millisecond where practical, comfortably under one second end-to-end. A witness measured within one order of magnitude of that hard cap is in MANDATORY migration territory. DERIVED, NOT MINTED (DESIGN §3 — no second hand-maintained number): the 500ms figure is gunbc_ci_fast_lane_eval_budget_ms() divided by witness_row_cost_migration_threshold_order_of_magnitude() (10, the 'one order of magnitude' the operator's own framing names); the two numbers cannot drift apart because there is only one authored constant. THE COMPARISON AXIS IS WALL (operator ruling 2026-08-05, witness_row_cost_clock_mismatch_note): the threshold is stated on the same wall eval column the receipt already carries, never thread CPU. THIS IS A DISCLOSURE GATE, not yet a wall — same shape as gunbc.ci_materialization's ci_floor_materialization_receipt_note: an unknown population sits over threshold today, and turning that into a merge-refusing gate on day one reds the fleet for work nobody has scheduled. What the disclosure gate DOES require, so it is not the coverage-by-illusion tier (DESIGN §6): every row is COUNTED and LOCATED (entry + declaration, never a bare aggregate), and it MUST be a live consumer of the existing per-witness cost receipt (gunbc.witness_row_cost's own N+1 ObservationEvent join) rather than new instrumentation. DECLARED PROMOTION TRIGGER (the carrier, not PR prose — DESIGN §5's escape-hatch rule applied to a disclosure: a trigger that lives only in a PR body is exactly the rediscovery generator this work exists to close): this disclosure promotes to a merge-refusing wall — new rows above threshold refuse admission — once witness_row_cost_mandatory_migration_count() reaches zero on a full-corpus falsifier run; the same burn-down-then-flip-to-hard-error shape gunbc.ci_materialization's duplicated_keys field already uses. Until that population is zero, EVERY existing over-threshold row must have a filed migration disposition (a located row on this or a successor roster) or the gate stays disclosure-only; a shrinking-but-nonzero population with untriaged rows is not evidence the trigger fired. SECOND, INDEPENDENT PROMOTION CONDITION (added per keen-ibex-349, 2026-08-05, from a measured ~2x observed-wall-clock spread across sessions on shared build-host jobserver contention — same witness, same tree, 2507-5291ms depending on ambient fleet load): promotion additionally requires that the observed axis this gate compares be a property of the witness, not of ambient fleet state at measurement time. A gate whose verdict flips with unrelated sessions' concurrent load is not fail-closed, it is nondeterministic, and 'nondeterministic' plus 'merge-blocking' produces exactly the rerun-as-remedy this project forbids (DESIGN §5). Today, disclosure-only, this costs a wobbling count and nothing else; it becomes load-bearing the moment the population-zero condition above is met. So BOTH conditions gate the flip to a wall: population zero AND a load-independent measurement axis (isolated/dedicated execution, a CPU-time rather than wall-clock measure, or an equivalent contention-insensitive observation) — satisfying only one is not evidence the trigger fired. First discriminating case (per the dispatching session): dag/test/claim/roadmap_program_view_witness_test.dag's four witnesses, root-caused to a cost-shape defect in gunbc.roadmap_program_view (per-node content_hash digests, unindexed parent lookups, O(n) depth folds) — this disclosure's mechanism is proven correct exactly when it surfaces that file as a located row, independent of whether any one witness happens to trip the 5s fail-stop."

fn witness_row_cost_migration_threshold_order_of_magnitude() -> std.nat.Nat {
  10
}

fn witness_row_cost_migration_threshold_ms() -> std.nat.Nat {
  gunbc_ci_fast_lane_eval_budget_ms() / witness_row_cost_migration_threshold_order_of_magnitude()
}

type MigrationDisclosureVerdict
  = BelowMigrationThreshold
  | MandatoryMigration { observed: std.measure.Millisecond, threshold: std.measure.Millisecond }

fn witness_row_cost_migration_verdict(observed: std.measure.Millisecond) -> gunbc.witness_row_cost.MigrationDisclosureVerdict {
  let threshold = millisecond(count: witness_row_cost_migration_threshold_ms())
  if millisecond_count(m: observed) >= millisecond_count(m: threshold) {
    MandatoryMigration { observed: observed, threshold: threshold }
  } else {
    BelowMigrationThreshold
  }
}

fn witness_row_cost_receipt_row_wall_eval_ms(row: gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostReceiptRow) -> Optional<std.measure.Millisecond> {
  match observation_duration_of(durations: row.eval, basis: WallClock) {
    MeasuredValue { value: v } => Present { value: nanosecond_to_millisecond_floor(n: v) }
    MeasuredUnavailable { cause: _ } => Absent
  }
}

fn witness_row_cost_receipt_row_migration_verdict(row: gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostReceiptRow) -> Optional<gunbc.witness_row_cost.MigrationDisclosureVerdict> {
  match witness_row_cost_receipt_row_wall_eval_ms(row: row) {
    Absent => Absent
    Present { value: observed } =>
      Present { value: witness_row_cost_migration_verdict(observed: observed) }
  }
}

fn witness_row_cost_mandatory_migration_rows(rows: List<gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostReceiptRow>) -> List<gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostReceiptRow> {
  filter(rows, row =>
    match witness_row_cost_receipt_row_migration_verdict(row: row) {
      Present { value: MandatoryMigration { observed: _, threshold: _ } } => true
      Present { value: BelowMigrationThreshold } => false
      Absent => false
    }
  )
}

fn witness_row_cost_mandatory_migration_count(rows: List<gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostReceiptRow>) -> Int {
  length(xs: witness_row_cost_mandatory_migration_rows(rows: rows))
}

data witness_row_cost_seed_verdict_note: String = "The seed's drift wire calls THESE, not the ratio predicate, so the verdict arms are named in exactly one place. Before this the seed called witness_row_cost_exceeds_basis and rebuilt DriftExceeded / WithinBasis / BasisAbsent as Rust string literals — a second representation of this module's sum, which is how a wall can exist here and not stand on the production path (the state witness_row_cost_clock_mismatch_note declared and this pair dissolves). The seed now reads the returned variant NAME and renders it, so adding an arm here reaches the receipt without a Rust edit, and a cross-clock pair refuses on the cadence exactly as it does in the witness. TWO ENTRY POINTS rather than an Optional crossing the boundary: the seed constructs no carrier, which is the same discipline witness_cost_timed_out_seed_deferral_note states for this seam, and whether a dated basis EXISTS is a map-lookup fact the seed already holds — it is presence of data, not a judgement about cost. The seed keeps deciding comparability first (its ObservationAbsent arm, for a row that never ran) because an absent OBSERVATION has no counterpart in this sum: there is nothing to judge, and inventing an arm for it here would let a row with no measurement reach a comparator at all. Dissolve-on: the same trigger as the rest of this seam, the witness-realization lane, where a realized runner holds the row and calls the verdict directly."

fn witness_row_cost_seed_verdict_dated(
  observed: std.measure.Millisecond,
  observed_clock: std.measure.ClockBasis,
  basis_clock: std.measure.ClockBasis,
  basis_eval: std.measure.Millisecond,
  run_ref: String,
) -> gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessRowCostVerdict {
  witness_row_cost_verdict(
    observed: observed,
    observed_clock: observed_clock,
    basis: Present {
      value: gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessRowCostDatedBasis {
        clock: basis_clock,
        eval: basis_eval,
        run_ref: run_ref
      }
    }
  )
}

fn witness_row_cost_seed_verdict_undated(
  observed: std.measure.Millisecond,
  observed_clock: std.measure.ClockBasis,
) -> gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessRowCostVerdict {
  witness_row_cost_verdict(
    observed: observed,
    observed_clock: observed_clock,
    basis: Absent
  )
}

fn witness_cost_warm(eval: std.measure.Nanosecond, resolve: std.measure.Nanosecond) -> std.measure.Nanosecond {
  nanosecond(count: nanosecond_count(n: eval) + nanosecond_count(n: resolve))
}

fn witness_cost_subject_entry(subject: std.observation.ObservationSubject) -> Optional<std.types.NonEmptyStr> {
  observation_subject_entry_path(subject: subject)
}

fn witness_cost_subject_declaration(subject: std.observation.ObservationSubject) -> Optional<std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef> {
  observation_subject_declaration(subject: subject)
}

fn witness_cost_measured_wall(wall: std.observation.Measured<std.measure.Nanosecond>) -> Optional<std.measure.Nanosecond> {
  match wall {
    MeasuredValue { value: v } => Present { value: v }
    MeasuredUnavailable { cause: _ } => Absent
  }
}

fn witness_cost_concluded_outcome(event: std.observation.ObservationEvent) -> Optional<std.observation.ObservationOutcome> {
  match event.transition {
    Concluded { outcome: o } => Present { value: o }
    Begin => Absent
    Step { k: _, n: _ } => Absent
  }
}

type WitnessCostEventRole
  = EntryResolveParent
  | DeclarationWitness

fn witness_cost_event_role(event: std.observation.ObservationEvent) -> Optional<gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostEventRole> {
  match witness_cost_concluded_outcome(event: event) {
    Absent => Absent
    Present { value: _ } =>
      match witness_cost_subject_entry(subject: event.subject) {
        Absent => Absent
        Present { value: _ } =>
          match observation_subject_grain(subject: event.subject) {
            DeclarationGrain => Present { value: DeclarationWitness }
            RunGrain => witness_cost_entry_resolve_role_if_shaped(subject: event.subject)
            BatchGrain => witness_cost_entry_resolve_role_if_shaped(subject: event.subject)
            EntryGrain => witness_cost_entry_resolve_role_if_shaped(subject: event.subject)
            ModuleGrain => Absent
            PhaseGrain => witness_cost_entry_resolve_role_if_shaped(subject: event.subject)
          }
      }
  }
}

fn witness_cost_entry_resolve_role_if_shaped(subject: std.observation.ObservationSubject) -> Optional<gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostEventRole> {
  match observation_subject_declaration(subject: subject) {
    Present { value: _ } => Absent
    Absent =>
      match observation_subject_module_path(subject: subject) {
        Present { value: _ } => Absent
        Absent =>
          match observation_subject_phase_name(subject: subject) {
            Absent => Absent
            Present { value: n } =>
              match n == ("resolve" as std.types.NonEmptyStr) {
                true => Present { value: EntryResolveParent }
                false => Absent
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn witness_cost_count_resolve_for_entry(events: List<std.observation.ObservationEvent>, entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr) -> std.nat.Nat {
  fold(events, init: 0, f: (acc, event) =>
    match witness_cost_event_role(event: event) {
      Present { value: EntryResolveParent } =>
        match witness_cost_subject_entry(subject: event.subject) {
          Present { value: p } =>
            if p == entry {
              acc + 1
            } else {
              acc
            }
          Absent => acc
        }
      Present { value: DeclarationWitness } => acc
      Absent => acc
    }
  )
}

fn witness_cost_count_declaration_for_entry(
  events: List<std.observation.ObservationEvent>,
  entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  declaration: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef,
) -> std.nat.Nat {
  fold(events, init: 0, f: (acc, event) =>
    match witness_cost_event_role(event: event) {
      Present { value: DeclarationWitness } =>
        match witness_cost_subject_entry(subject: event.subject) {
          Present { value: p } =>
            match witness_cost_subject_declaration(subject: event.subject) {
              Present { value: d } =>
                if p == entry && d == declaration {
                  acc + 1
                } else {
                  acc
                }
              Absent => acc
            }
          Absent => acc
        }
      Present { value: EntryResolveParent } => acc
      Absent => acc
    }
  )
}

fn witness_cost_unique_resolve_wall(
  events: List<std.observation.ObservationEvent>,
  entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
) -> Optional<std.measure.Nanosecond> {
  if witness_cost_count_resolve_for_entry(events: events, entry: entry) == 1 {
    fold(events, init: Absent, f: (acc, event) =>
      match witness_cost_event_role(event: event) {
        Present { value: EntryResolveParent } =>
          match witness_cost_subject_entry(subject: event.subject) {
            Present { value: p } =>
              if p == entry {
                witness_cost_measured_wall(wall: observation_wall(event: event))
              } else {
                acc
              }
            Absent => acc
          }
        Present { value: DeclarationWitness } => acc
        Absent => acc
      }
    )
  } else {
    Absent
  }
}

type WitnessCostReceiptRow {
  entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr
  declaration: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef
  eval: List<std.observation.TimedMeasurement>
  resolve: std.measure.Nanosecond
  warm: std.measure.Nanosecond
  outcome: std.observation.ObservationOutcome
}

data witness_cost_row_eval_clocks_note: String = "The row's eval term carries EVERY clock the occurrence was measured on, each tagged, rather than one untagged number. WALL IS REQUIRED AND CPU IS NOT: the projection still refuses a witness whose wall figure is MeasuredUnavailable, because wall is what the witness threshold is stated on (operator ruling 2026-08-05) and a threshold with no measurement is the fabricated-plausible-output DESIGN section 5 forbids. Thread CPU rides beside it as the REMEDY DISCRIMINATOR, which is the only reason a second clock earns a column: a slow row with high CPU is algorithm or repeated evaluation, a slow row with low CPU is waiting, I/O, subprocess or scheduling, and one clock cannot tell an author which fix applies. Its absence is therefore an honest gap in what a producer sampled, not a refusal — the seed's run_claim_measured reads both from one run so production rows carry both, while a producer that genuinely sampled only wall says so by omission and observation_duration_of answers MeasuredUnavailable with that cause rather than a zero. The resolve and warm terms stay bare Nanosecond and stay WALL: an entry resolve is wall by construction (it is dominated by I/O the CPU clock cannot see) and witness_decomposition_does_not_reduce_entry_cost_note is the standing ruling that it is a different grain from this row entirely."


type WitnessCostProjection
  = WitnessCostProjectionComplete { rows: List<gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostReceiptRow> }
  | WitnessCostProjectionRefused { cause: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

fn witness_cost_project_one_witness(
  events: List<std.observation.ObservationEvent>,
  event: std.observation.ObservationEvent,
) -> gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostProjection {
  match witness_cost_subject_entry(subject: event.subject) {
    Absent =>
      WitnessCostProjectionRefused {
        cause: "declaration-grain witness event missing EntrySegment" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
      }
    Present { value: entry } => {
      let n = witness_cost_count_resolve_for_entry(events: events, entry: entry)
      if n == 0 {
        WitnessCostProjectionRefused {
          cause: "missing parent resolve-phase event for entry (refuse; never invent)" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
        }
      } else if n > 1 {
        WitnessCostProjectionRefused {
          cause: "multiple parent resolve-phase events for entry (refuse; never silently pick)" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
        }
      } else {
        match witness_cost_unique_resolve_wall(events: events, entry: entry) {
          Absent =>
            WitnessCostProjectionRefused {
              cause: "parent resolve wall MeasuredUnavailable (refuse; never fabricate)" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
            }
          Present { value: resolve } =>
            match witness_cost_measured_wall(wall: observation_wall(event: event)) {
              Absent =>
                WitnessCostProjectionRefused {
                  cause: "witness eval wall MeasuredUnavailable (refuse; never fabricate)" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
                }
              Present { value: eval_wall } =>
                match witness_cost_subject_declaration(subject: event.subject) {
                  Absent =>
                    WitnessCostProjectionRefused {
                      cause: "declaration-grain witness missing DeclarationSegment" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
                    }
                  Present { value: decl } =>
                    if witness_cost_count_declaration_for_entry(
                      events: events,
                      entry: entry,
                      declaration: decl
                    ) != 1 {
                      WitnessCostProjectionRefused {
                        cause: "duplicate declaration witness identity for entry (refuse; never silently duplicate/overwrite)" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
                      }
                    } else {
                      match witness_cost_concluded_outcome(event: event) {
                        Absent =>
                          WitnessCostProjectionRefused {
                            cause: "declaration-grain witness is not Concluded" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
                          }
                        Present { value: outcome } =>
                          WitnessCostProjectionComplete {
                            rows: [
                              WitnessCostReceiptRow {
                                entry: entry,
                                declaration: decl,
                                eval: event.durations,
                                resolve: resolve,
                                warm: witness_cost_warm(eval: eval_wall, resolve: resolve),
                                outcome: outcome
                              }
                            ]
                          }
                      }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn witness_cost_append_projection(
  left: gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostProjection,
  right: gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostProjection,
) -> gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostProjection {
  match left {
    WitnessCostProjectionRefused { cause: c } => WitnessCostProjectionRefused { cause: c }
    WitnessCostProjectionComplete { rows: a } =>
      match right {
        WitnessCostProjectionRefused { cause: c } => WitnessCostProjectionRefused { cause: c }
        WitnessCostProjectionComplete { rows: b } =>
          WitnessCostProjectionComplete { rows: concat(a, b) }
      }
  }
}

fn witness_cost_check_unique_resolve_parent(
  events: List<std.observation.ObservationEvent>,
  event: std.observation.ObservationEvent,
) -> gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostProjection {
  match witness_cost_subject_entry(subject: event.subject) {
    Absent =>
      WitnessCostProjectionRefused {
        cause: "entry resolve parent missing EntrySegment" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
      }
    Present { value: entry } => {
      let n = witness_cost_count_resolve_for_entry(events: events, entry: entry)
      match n == 1 {
        true => WitnessCostProjectionComplete { rows: [] }
        false =>
          WitnessCostProjectionRefused {
            cause: "multiple parent resolve-phase events for an entry (refuse; never silently pick)" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn project_witness_cost_receipt(events: List<std.observation.ObservationEvent>) -> gunbc.witness_row_cost.WitnessCostProjection {
  fold(events, init: WitnessCostProjectionComplete { rows: [] }, f: (acc, event) =>
    match acc {
      WitnessCostProjectionRefused { cause: c } =>
        WitnessCostProjectionRefused { cause: c }
      WitnessCostProjectionComplete { rows: _ } =>
        match witness_cost_event_role(event: event) {
          Present { value: DeclarationWitness } =>
            witness_cost_append_projection(
              left: acc,
              right: witness_cost_project_one_witness(events: events, event: event)
            )
          Present { value: EntryResolveParent } =>
            witness_cost_append_projection(
              left: acc,
              right: witness_cost_check_unique_resolve_parent(events: events, event: event)
            )
          Absent =>
            WitnessCostProjectionRefused {
              cause: "malformed or non-concluded witness timing event (refuse; never drop)" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
            }
        }
    }
  )
}

fn witness_cost_rss_unavailable_cause() -> std.types.NonEmptyStr {
  "per-witness RSS not sampled on discovery claim path" as std.types.NonEmptyStr
}

fn witness_cost_entry_resolve_event(
  entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  resolve: std.measure.Nanosecond,
) -> std.observation.ObservationEvent {
  std.observation.ObservationEvent {
    subject: std.observation.ObservationSubject {
      segments: [
        std.observation.EntrySegment { source_path: entry },
        std.observation.PhaseSegment { name: "resolve" as std.types.NonEmptyStr }
      ]
    },
    transition: std.observation.Concluded { outcome: Done },
    durations: observation_durations_wall(elapsed: resolve),
    rss: std.observation.MeasuredUnavailable { cause: witness_cost_rss_unavailable_cause() }
  }
}

fn witness_cost_nested_resolve_under_declaration_event(
  entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  declaration: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef,
  resolve: std.measure.Nanosecond,
) -> std.observation.ObservationEvent {
  std.observation.ObservationEvent {
    subject: std.observation.ObservationSubject {
      segments: [
        std.observation.EntrySegment { source_path: entry },
        std.observation.DeclarationSegment { declaration: declaration },
        std.observation.PhaseSegment { name: "resolve" as std.types.NonEmptyStr }
      ]
    },
    transition: std.observation.Concluded { outcome: Done },
    durations: observation_durations_wall(elapsed: resolve),
    rss: std.observation.MeasuredUnavailable { cause: witness_cost_rss_unavailable_cause() }
  }
}

fn witness_cost_module_resolve_event(
  entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  module_path: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  resolve: std.measure.Nanosecond,
) -> std.observation.ObservationEvent {
  std.observation.ObservationEvent {
    subject: std.observation.ObservationSubject {
      segments: [
        std.observation.EntrySegment { source_path: entry },
        std.observation.ModuleSegment { module_path: module_path },
        std.observation.PhaseSegment { name: "resolve" as std.types.NonEmptyStr }
      ]
    },
    transition: std.observation.Concluded { outcome: Done },
    durations: observation_durations_wall(elapsed: resolve),
    rss: std.observation.MeasuredUnavailable { cause: witness_cost_rss_unavailable_cause() }
  }
}

fn witness_cost_declaration_event(
  entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  declaration: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef,
  eval: List<std.observation.TimedMeasurement>,
  outcome: std.observation.ObservationOutcome,
) -> std.observation.ObservationEvent {
  std.observation.ObservationEvent {
    subject: std.observation.ObservationSubject {
      segments: [
        std.observation.EntrySegment { source_path: entry },
        std.observation.DeclarationSegment { declaration: declaration }
      ]
    },
    transition: std.observation.Concluded { outcome: outcome },
    durations: eval,
    rss: std.observation.MeasuredUnavailable { cause: witness_cost_rss_unavailable_cause() }
  }
}

fn witness_cost_seed_declaration(
  module_path: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  function: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
) -> std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef {
  std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef {
    module_path: module_path,
    decl_name: function,
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

fn witness_cost_seed_done_event(
  entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  module_path: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  function: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  eval: List<std.observation.TimedMeasurement>,
) -> std.observation.ObservationEvent {
  witness_cost_declaration_event(
    entry: entry,
    declaration: witness_cost_seed_declaration(module_path: module_path, function: function),
    eval: eval,
    outcome: Done
  )
}

fn witness_cost_seed_refused_event(
  entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  module_path: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  function: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  eval: List<std.observation.TimedMeasurement>,
  diagnostic: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
) -> std.observation.ObservationEvent {
  witness_cost_declaration_event(
    entry: entry,
    declaration: witness_cost_seed_declaration(module_path: module_path, function: function),
    eval: eval,
    outcome: std.observation.Refused { diagnostic: diagnostic }
  )
}

fn witness_cost_seed_failed_event(
  entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  module_path: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  function: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  eval: List<std.observation.TimedMeasurement>,
  error: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
) -> std.observation.ObservationEvent {
  witness_cost_declaration_event(
    entry: entry,
    declaration: witness_cost_seed_declaration(module_path: module_path, function: function),
    eval: eval,
    outcome: std.observation.Failed { error: error, output: "" }
  )
}

data witness_cost_seed_timed_out_note: String = "A deadline-killed witness is TimedOut, never Failed and never Done. The distinction is not cosmetic: a killed row's recorded wall is its CEILING, not its cost — a censored measurement — so anything that reads it as a completed duration is reading a fabricated value. The concrete price was paid twice. The falsifier alert reported the whole run red and erased its green components (gunbc.floor_component_receipt), and the witness row-cost basis could not seed the corpus's most expensive row at all: resolution_divergence_silent_pick_gate_keystone_holds was killed at 900000ms but recorded a flat 900794ms, and seeding that under the 2x comparator would have pinned the row WithinBasis below ~1.8M ms forever, enshrining a deadline ceiling as normal cost. Upstream of this constructor the seed used to flatten the typed budget pair into a message string and the floor then recovered the classification by substring-matching that same prose (falsifier_failure_mode's .contains checks), whose fallback arm is WitnessRed — so rewording an error silently reclassified a budget refusal as a witness failure. Both budget clocks route here: cpu (thread CPU, the stride-poll metric) and wall (whole-receipt, counting subprocess I/O) are different states with different remedies, so the seed keeps them apart as BudgetKind and surfaces the label in the failure detail and the batch failure mode. THAT DEFERRAL IS NOW DISCHARGED (2026-08-05, operator clock-basis ruling). This note used to say the clock was deliberately NOT a field on this event, because extending ObservationOutcome.TimedOut meant changing load-bearing substrate that lane held no mandate for, and it named the exact condition for revisiting: if a consumer needs the clock from the event itself, take the field to std.observation rather than widening it here. A consumer did, and the field went there. TimedOut now carries basis: ClockBasis, this constructor takes it as a parameter, and the seed passes the BudgetKind it already keeps rather than surfacing the clock only in prose. An ObservationEvent alone now DOES say which clock expired. Parameters are Millisecond carriers, not bare Nat: the caller is what can get a unit wrong, and the seed constructs the carrier by calling millisecond(count:) across the boundary so the constructor stays the single authority."

data witness_cost_timed_out_seed_deferral_note: String = "PURE BOOTSTRAP RECEIPT for the seed-side Rust this constructor is fed by (review 45226 asked for it, correctly, and the sibling gunbc.floor_component_receipt seed_transport_deferral_note is the precedent it matches). WHAT IS ADDED: no new Rust file and no parallel authority — a variant on the existing ClaimOutcome sum in the already-seed-retained cli_run module (TimedOut carrying elapsed_ms/budget_ms/kind), the BudgetKind label pair, a BudgetRefusal projection field on ClaimResult so the fact survives the flattening into ok/detail, and discovery_budget_refusal which lifts that fact out of a discovery batch's witness_outcomes. WHAT IT REPLACES rather than accumulates: the two format!-into-RuntimeError sites and the reliance on falsifier_failure_mode's substring recovery of the classification — the change is net-negative on the seed's judgment surface, since the seed no longer decides what a budget message MEANS by reading its own prose. WHAT IS NOT IN RUST: the outcome vocabulary and the receipt shape, which are std.observation's ObservationOutcome and this module's projection; the seed constructs no carrier, calling millisecond(count:) through the interpreter and handing the resulting Millisecond back, so no flat-scalar duration crosses the seam. DEFERRED TO: the v1 exit finish line ZeroHandMaintainedRust, roadmap node v1-zero-hand-maintained-rust (gunbc.v1_deletion_plan v1_exit_finish_lines). CONCRETE DISSOLUTION: this transport deletes when witness execution is realized from .dag rather than interpreted in the seed bin (the witness-realization lane — emit witnesses to native code and schedule the runs through realization/materialization), at which point the budget deadline is enforced by the realized runner and the TimedOut event is constructed here directly instead of being marshalled across the interpreter boundary. Note this is a DIFFERENT trigger from witness_cost_seed_timed_out_note's, which concerns adding the clock kind to std.observation; that one widens the modeled outcome, this one deletes the seed transport, and neither implies the other."

data host_dependency_refusal_seed_deferral_note: String = "PURE BOOTSTRAP RECEIPT for the seed-side Rust that lifts HostDependencyAbsent off flattened discovery and single-claim outcomes (review 51813 asked for it, correctly — sibling to witness_cost_timed_out_seed_deferral_note on the same ClaimResult seam). WHAT IS ADDED: no new Rust file and no parallel authority — HostDependencyRefusal, host_dependency_refusal_from_detail parsing the failure-receipt wire, host_dependency_refusal on ClaimResult, discovery_host_dependency_refusal lifting the first wire out of summary.failures, and the batch_failure_mode_and_detail arm that reads the field instead of falsifier_failure_mode substring recovery. WHAT IT REPLACES rather than accumulates: npm-absent Codex wet failures on falsifier batch 5 (RunnableDiscoveryBatch) falling through to WitnessRed because the failure-receipt wire lived only in detail prose — the change is net-negative on judgment surface, since the seed no longer classifies host obligation from its own formatted string. WHAT IS NOT IN RUST: observe_host_cli_dependency, host_dependency_absent_wire, codex_wet_materialization_host_cli_requirements enrollment, and package_delivery's pre-transport refusal — all in gunbc.host_cli_dependency and gunbc.package_delivery; the seed only unmarshals the wire the witness already emitted. DEFERRED TO: the v1 exit finish line ZeroHandMaintainedRust, roadmap node v1-zero-hand-maintained-rust (gunbc.v1_deletion_plan v1_exit_finish_lines). CONCRETE DISSOLUTION: this transport deletes when witness execution is realized from .dag rather than interpreted in the seed bin (the witness-realization lane), at which point HostCliDependencyAbsent survives the interpreter boundary as a typed outcome and batch classification reads it directly instead of re-parsing HostDependencyAbsent\{tool=...\} from detail."

fn witness_cost_seed_timed_out_event(
  entry: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  module_path: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  function: std.types.NonEmptyStr,
  eval: List<std.observation.TimedMeasurement>,
  basis: std.measure.ClockBasis,
  budget: std.measure.Millisecond,
  elapsed: std.measure.Millisecond,
) -> std.observation.ObservationEvent {
  witness_cost_declaration_event(
    entry: entry,
    declaration: witness_cost_seed_declaration(module_path: module_path, function: function),
    eval: eval,
    outcome: std.observation.TimedOut { basis: basis, budget: budget, elapsed: elapsed }
  )
}

// Re-homed here from v2.workflow.ci_floor_plan when the CI wipe (2026-08-15) deleted that module.
// The five-second fast-lane budget is a fact about what a witness row may cost, which is this
// module's subject; it sat in the floor plan only because the floor plan was what armed it. No
// consumer arms it today -- the executor that did is deleted -- so this is the threshold's
// declaration, not a claim that anything enforces it.
//
// THE NAMES ARE PRESERVED ACROSS THE MOVE, DELIBERATELY. The first version of this re-home renamed
// them to witness_row_fast_lane_*, which typechecked and read tidier and broke 65 citations in one
// motion -- every dissolution row in gunbc.ci_layer_roots that names the budget it must fall under,
// plus the migration-threshold note and its witness. DESIGN §3 rules that a citation names the
// SYMBOL, precisely so it survives a move; a rename during a move is the one edit that defeats
// that, and it defeats it silently, because no citation is checked. A move keeps the name.
data gunbc_ci_fast_lane_witness_eval_budget: std.measure.Second = second(count: 5)

fn gunbc_ci_fast_lane_eval_budget_ms() -> v2.std.nat.Nat {
  second_count(s: gunbc_ci_fast_lane_witness_eval_budget) * 1000
}

data gunbc_ci_fast_lane_rule_note: String = "Fast-lane 5-second rule (operator ruling 2026-07-12). RE-HOMED HERE 2026-08-15 from v2.workflow.ci_floor_plan, deleted by the CI wipe, and CORRECTED IN THE SAME MOTION rather than restored verbatim — the original text asserted an enforcement mechanism this cut removed, and re-homing it unchanged would have carried a false claim into a live carrier under a name 25 other rows cite. WHAT SURVIVES: a witness whose OWN eval takes 5 seconds or longer does not run in per-PR discovery — it lives under a long/ test dir (dag/test/claim/long/, src/v2/test/claim/long/), excluded from discovery at dir grain (gunbc.ci_layer_roots long_lane_exclusion_note) and triggered independently (local recipe: claim_batch --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry <file> --functions <csv>). The threshold itself is gunbc_ci_fast_lane_witness_eval_budget above, this module's own row. WHAT DIED, STATED PLAINLY: enforcement. The original read 'Enforcement is by execution, not census: the executor arms a cooperative per-witness eval deadline from the budget row (v1_interpreter EvalBudgetExceeded — a typed FAIL row naming the over-budget witness; the bound unwinds from INSIDE eval because in-process worker threads have no kill authority, the Phase A governor lesson)', and the executor that armed it is deleted. NO CONSUMER ARMS THIS BUDGET TODAY. So the dir policy is a placement convention with no execution behind it, and an over-budget witness in per-PR discovery now runs to completion instead of failing red in seconds with its name in the log. THE RISK THAT RE-OPENS, kept because it is the receipt that justified the deadline and it is exactly what is unguarded again: an unbounded eval previously burned the 270-minute step cap in billed silence (run 29183446733: 243 of 270 minutes after the last progress line, zero witnesses completed). RUNG: this class drops from mechanically preventable to unguarded for the duration of the cut, declared here rather than left to be rediscovered by a runner that hangs. RESTORATION TRIGGER: the re-added floor arms a per-witness eval deadline from this row again, at which point this paragraph deletes and the enforcement sentence returns. Dissolve-on (unchanged, and it outlives the restoration): per-witness declared cost envelopes (the witness-cost-locality admission law) replace the blanket lane budget with declared rows."

// ── THE SHARED FILL: who paid for a computation, and who benefited ───────────────────────────
//
// witness_decomposition_does_not_reduce_entry_cost_note above rules that per-witness eval and
// per-entry resolve are different terms. This is the same distinction one layer down, and it
// reaches INSIDE the eval column the note treats as the row's own: a witness that is the first
// to reach a process-global corpus computation is charged its entire fill, and every later
// witness reaching the same computation is charged nothing. So the eval figure is not always a
// property of the row it names, and the population that reads highest is partly a list of who
// ran first.
data witness_shared_fill_note: String = "A SHARED FILL IS PAID ONCE AND READ FREE, SO THE ROW CHARGED FOR IT IS NOT THE ROW THAT COSTS IT. Measured on gunbc#8455: two claims in one witness file, 35ms apart, calling the same projection, cost 12197ms and 31ms — 393x for one call, because the first paid an O(corpus) parse pass that the second read out of a process-global cache. The population behind it is the seed's corpus-scan caches (reference edges, module path index, module graph facts, and the corpus census reports), each keyed on its declared roots and filled at most once per process. WHY THIS IS A MEASUREMENT DEFECT AND NOT A COST DEFECT: nothing is computed twice — the caches are correct and the sharing is the saving. What is wrong is the attribution, and it is wrong in the direction that matters, because the floor's ceiling refuses per row. A refusal therefore names the first toucher, whose own cost may be milliseconds, while the computation it was refused for stays on the floor. TWO CONSEQUENCES BIND ANY PARING DECISION. Splitting such a row cannot help: the charge moves to whichever fragment runs first, which is witness_decomposition_does_not_reduce_entry_cost_note observed rather than predicted. And REMOVING such a row does not remove its cost either — the next claim to touch the same fill pays the same seconds, so a quarantine that reads the per-row number can trade a named refusal for an unnamed one in a module that was fast before. THE QUANTITY A REMOVAL DECISION NEEDS is therefore marginal, not attributed: removing a module saves its rows' exclusive time plus the fills whose consumers all live inside it, and saves nothing of a fill any other module also reads. shared_fill_disposition is that classification, and it is scoped to ONE RUN: SharedFillExclusiveToOneModule means no other module in the executed population read that fill, not that none ever would — if the payer is removed a would-be consumer may pay it next run, which is the same first-touch trap one level up. What bounds the doubt is that a fill is keyed on its DECLARED INPUTS, so an exclusive fill on a key no other consumer requests is exclusive by construction rather than by luck; and the residue is decidable by execution, by running the fold with the payer absent and reading the ledger again. Until such a run exists, a recovery estimated from an exclusive row is an upper bound. the seed's cli_run shared_fill ledger is its producer, and it reports every fill's payer and consumer breadth so the marginal figure is derivable rather than assumed."

type SharedFillPayer
  = SharedFillPaidByClaim { qualified: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | SharedFillPaidOutsideFold

// THE THREE STATES ARE THREE REMEDIES, which is why this is not a Bool beside a payer.
// Outside-fold cost belongs to preparation and no witness can be pared to recover it; an
// exclusive fill is genuinely removable with its module; a shared fill survives the removal of
// any single consumer. Collapsing the first into either of the others is the state-space
// conflation DESIGN §5 names — preparation cost wearing a witness's name is exactly how a
// removal comes to be justified by a saving it cannot deliver.
type SharedFillDisposition
  = SharedFillOutsideFold
  | SharedFillExclusiveToOneModule
  | SharedFillSharedAcrossModules

// `consumer_modules` counts DISTINCT modules that touched this fill, payer included. One means
// the fill lives and dies with that module; more means it does not. Zero is only reachable for a
// fill paid outside the fold, and is answered by the payer arm before the count is consulted, so
// no arm has to invent a meaning for "nobody touched a computation that happened".
fn shared_fill_disposition(
  payer: gunbc.witness_row_cost.SharedFillPayer,
  consumer_modules: std.nat.Nat,
) -> gunbc.witness_row_cost.SharedFillDisposition {
  match payer {
    SharedFillPaidOutsideFold => SharedFillOutsideFold
    SharedFillPaidByClaim { qualified: _ } =>
      if consumer_modules > 1 {
        SharedFillSharedAcrossModules
      } else {
        SharedFillExclusiveToOneModule
      }
  }
}

fn shared_fill_disposition_tag(disposition: gunbc.witness_row_cost.SharedFillDisposition) -> String {
  match disposition {
    SharedFillOutsideFold => "outside-fold"
    SharedFillExclusiveToOneModule => "exclusive"
    SharedFillSharedAcrossModules => "shared"
  }
}

fn shared_fill_payer_tag(payer: gunbc.witness_row_cost.SharedFillPayer) -> String {
  match payer {
    SharedFillPaidOutsideFold => "<outside-fold>"
    SharedFillPaidByClaim { qualified: q } => q
  }
}
